				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Eh, its a PROBABLE dead-on-arrival CPU. Just figured I'd ask and see if anybody else agrees with me:
<br />

<br />
The CPU in question is an Athlon XP 2400 (1.93 GHz, 266 FSB). My mainboard is an Asus A7V8X-X, which says it'll handle up to a 2.25 GHz+ CPU and can accept 333 FSB processors. So it should have no issues, right?
<br />

<br />
Well, with the new CPU and fan installed, my system will power on for all of 2 seconds, then immediately shut off. The power in the system is actually drained: the computer won't even turn back on for that two seconds unless I flip the voltage switch on the pow supply or reseat the power cord.
<br />

<br />
This is NOT a low power issue: I have a 300W pow supply, and disconnecting all other components in the system makes no nevermind. Seeing as how I have sold systems with bigger processors that ran off a 150W pow supply, I somehow don't think the CPU requires more than 300 all by itself <img src="images/smiles/icon_wink.gif" alt="" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
My overheat alert in BIOS is set to 95 degrees celsius (yeowch), so its definitely not a heating issue either - even without the fan it'd take it longer than it does to reach that temperature.
<br />

<br />
When I put the original CPU back in the system, it boots right back up.
<br />

<br />
Anybody have any other suggestions I can try, outside of calling the folks I bought it from and getting a refund?<br />_________________<br />Beld Demonblade, retired
